properties.statement:
READ FROM SOURCE 2026-08-10 while starting BL-198: reflow2's schema YAML declares 81 `default:` values across 39 node types, and **dynograph-core APPLIES THEM AT WRITE TIME**. `dynograph-core/src/schema.rs:507`, in the validation path every upsert goes through:

    for (prop_name, prop_def) in &node_def.properties {
        if !properties.contains_key(prop_name) && let Some(default) = &prop_def.default {
            properties.insert(prop_name.clone(), default.clone());
        }

So a caller who says nothing gets the default STORED. That is why `add_capability` never mentions `tier` and every Capability nonetheless carries `tier: operational`, and why 3,207 stored values across 1,178 of 1,980 nodes — 59% of this design — equal a schema-declared default.

⚠️ THIS NODE REPLACES A WRONG ONE I RECORDED TEN MINUTES EARLIER, `fact:the-schema-declares-81-defaults-and-nothing-reads-them`, which claimed the declarations were parsed by nobody. DELETED RATHER THAN RETIRED, because it was never true (retire-from-design's one question). The error: I grepped dynograph's schema.rs for `default`, saw a screenful of `#[serde(default)]` attributes in the head of the output, and concluded the field did not exist. `pub default: Option<Value>` is on line 127 of that same file. A confident answer from an incomplete look — the session's own recurring failure, arrived at for the fourth time.

⭐ THE CORRECTION IMPROVES THE PLAN RATHER THAN MERELY FIXING IT. `default:` in the YAML is not documentation, it is an INSTRUCTION TO INJECT. So BL-198's "stop storing a value nobody chose" has a route that needs no code change and crosses no repo boundary: **REMOVE THE `default:` KEY from the properties where injection is wrong.** dynograph then injects nothing, the property is genuinely absent, and absence honestly means nobody said.

THE CONSEQUENCE THAT MAKES THIS CHEAP: the change is FORWARD-ONLY. Existing nodes keep the values already stored, so the committed export does not churn and `compare_designs` sees nothing — the phantom-diff cost BL-198 warned about, and that step 2 of `dec:fix-the-comparison-before-pointing-it-earlier` exists to disclose, DOES NOT ARISE for this route at all. Removing a `default:` costs one YAML line per property and no migration.

WHAT STILL NEEDS THE OWNER'S JUDGEMENT, per property: whether the value means "nobody said" or is a MEANINGFUL INITIAL STATE. `Requirement.status = proposed` is the latter — the whole certainty doctrine reads it (`dec:certainty-derived`, graph_report's certainty line, detect_gaps, loop_status, what_next), and removing its default would break derivation rather than clean it. The candidates that carry no information are the descriptive enums nobody chooses: Requirement.kind/concern/lineage/designation, Capability.tier, Component.kind, Fragment.fragment_type.

AND A SECOND-ORDER QUESTION THIS OPENS: with the `default:` removed, where does the CONVENTIONAL value get stated for a reader who wants to know what absence implies? It cannot stay in the key dynograph consumes. Either reflow2 keeps its own table, or the value stops being conventional at all — which for a descriptive enum nobody chooses is arguably the honest answer.

properties.description:
The check itself in `tools/skill_lint.py` (run by CI), plus 4 cases in `tools/test_skill_lint.py` guarding the check from rotting.

🛑 OBSERVED FAILING FIRST, against the unfixed tree, exit 1: `named in NO skill: ['consumption_report', 'flow_report', 'ility_report', 'maturity_report', 'readiness_report', 'release_report']` — six of fourteen. Recorded because a failing-first test and one written after the fix both end green, and the difference only shows when the class recurs.

THE FOUR UNIT CASES GUARD THE CHECK, NOT THE ROUTING, because the check is the only thing keeping the routing true and a check that quietly stopped finding anything would look exactly like a design with nothing left to route:
① the parse actually finds reports (≥10, and `graph_report`/`maturity_report` among them) — the vacuous-pass failure `served_tools` already refuses with its own floor;
② every exemption carries a non-trivial reason, so the register cannot become a place to put anything inconvenient;
③ every exemption says WHICH KIND it is (`GAP:` or `needs no skill`) — a routing hole that reads as settled never gets fixed;
④ the register names only tools that are actually served, so a stale entry cannot silently exempt nothing while looking like a decision.

⚠️ WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK: that anyone READS the report. Naming is reachability, not use.

`the_root_cause_skill_forces_the_steps_that_stop_a_premature_fix`, in crates/reflow2-mcp/tests/served_skills.rs. Four assertions, each pinning a clause whose removal would turn the skill back into prose somebody nods along with rather than executes:

① it demands a measurement that could REFUTE a candidate, not merely confirm one;
② it states that three candidate causes is a FLOOR rather than a target;
③ it names the calls that GENERATE candidates from the design (`granularity_report`, `coverage_report`), because "think of three causes" without them yields two real candidates and a filler;
④ it names `rule:an-issue-is-root-caused-then-pinned-by-a-test-then-fixed`, so the procedure and the rule carrying the user's word cannot drift apart unnoticed.

⭐ ONE ASSERTION WAS DELIBERATELY LOOSENED, AND THE REASON IS RECORDED IN THE TEST ITSELF. It first pinned the literal string "THREE" and fired on an edit that moved the word into a sentence while keeping the requirement exactly. It now matches "three is a floor" case-insensitively. A test that fails on casing teaches its author to weaken it, which is how a pin stops pinning anything — so the fix was to assert the PROPERTY rather than the formatting.

⚠️ WHAT IT CANNOT CHECK: whether an agent actually follows the steps. It pins that the instruction still says what it said; nothing here observes a session obeying it. The wider byte-identity and command-existence checks (`ver:served-skills`, `ver:every-skill-has-a-command`) cover this skill automatically as they do every other.

crates/reflow2-core/tests/vocabulary_coverage_reports_what_was_never_used.rs, plus one cross-file tripwire in tests/internal_only_delivery.rs.

THE ORIGINAL FIVE, one decision each: figures always / list only on request; the share computed so the reader is not left dividing; grouped by the schema's own domains, ordered worst-covered first, with ALL eleven reported so a missing domain cannot read as fully used; a domain parkable ONLY by an ACCEPTED ruling, with the remedy stated on the finding; and the barely-started note firing under ten nodes and standing down above.

FIVE MORE, 2026-08-22, for the properties the list now NAMES rather than only counts:
  an unused property is named, node and edge alike, and the naming still rides the WITHHELD list;
  a property is named ONLY on a type that has instances — the vacuous-zero rule applied to the names as strictly as to the figures;
  one instance carrying a property makes it used, without taking the rest of the type down with it;
  a DEFAULTED property can never be named, because the store materialises defaults on write — the honest limit, pinned so nobody upgrades the claim;
  property names come out sorted within their type.

🛑 THAT LAST PROBE WAS WRONG ON ITS FIRST WRITING AND THE SECOND VERSION IS THE POINT. It compared two calls and asserted they matched. The schema is a process-wide OnceLock, so both calls walk the same HashMap with the same seed and agree whether or not anything is sorted — an assertion that could only ever pass. Order has to be checked against something outside the map, so it now asserts ascending property names within each type, which a dropped `names.sort()` kills.

AND THE CROSS-FILE TRIPWIRE INVERTED. `nothing_classified_yields_no_finding_but_the_silence_is_now_reportable` used to assert that `vocabulary_coverage` did NOT name `Artifact.audience`, holding the hole open on the record. It now asserts that it DOES — and separately that the default reply still withholds the list, so 'closed' cannot be quietly rounded up from 'reportable'.

FOUR MUTATIONS RUN AGAINST THE NEW CODE, each killed by exactly its own probe and no other: dropped `names.sort()`; properties named on types with no instances; the property counted but not named (the original hole, restored — killed by the cross-file tripwire); the per-domain figure leaking out of the totals.
